ok - install-boot writes a foreground unit pinned to the home's own script, enables it, and turns lingering on
ok - install-boot refuses a linked worktree home and names the primary checkout to pass, leaving real checkouts and plain homes alone
ok - a trailing slash, a symlink, a space or a percent sign in the home never splits it from its own unit
ok - a refused enable-linger still installs and enables the unit, and names the command to run by hand
ok - once the unit manages the home, start, stop, restart and server-status all speak for the unit
ok - restart retires the hand-started board and hands its address to the unit, losing nothing
ok - a unit that names another home leaves this home's pidfile lifecycle exactly as it was
ok - uninstall-boot stops, disables and removes the unit, and the home goes back to the pidfile lifecycle
